<PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="MEDLINE" Owner="NLM" IndexingMethod="Curated"><PMID Version="1">42836590</PMID><Article PubModel="Print"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">2050-4527</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><Volume>14</Volume><Issue>10</Issue><PubDate><Year>2026</Year><Month>Oct</Month></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Immunity, inflammation and disease</Title><ISOAbbreviation>Immun Inflamm Dis</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Impact of Liver Metastases on Outcomes With Chemotherapy or Immune Checkpoint Inhibitors in Pancreatic Cancer.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>e70545</StartPage><MedlinePgn>e70545</MedlinePgn></Pagination><Abstract><AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Pancreatic cancer (PC) is on the rise worldwide, with significant improvements in survival rates still awaited. This study aimed to evaluate the impact of LM on outcomes in patients with advanced PC treated with chemotherapy or immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICIs).</AbstractText><AbstractText Label="MATERIAL AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We performed post-hoc survival analyses using data from a chemotherapy-based study (n&#x2009;=&#x2009;147) and ICI-based studies (n&#x2009;=&#x2009;129). mRNA and lncRNA levels were not assessed.</AbstractText><AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">In the chemotherapy cohort, overall survival was significantly lower in patients with LM (7.0&#x2009;months, 95% CI: 6.0-8.8; log-rank p&#x2009;=&#x2009;0.002).</AbstractText><AbstractText Label="DISCUSSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">LM were associated with significantly shorter OS and PFS in patients with PC receiving chemotherapy.</AbstractText><CopyrightInformation>&#xa9; 2026 The Author(s). Published by John Wiley &amp; Sons Ltd.</CopyrightInformation></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Kj&#xe6;r</LastName><ForeName>Laura K</ForeName><Initials>LK</Initials></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Theile</LastN